lex:tapi-streaming-legacy-properties
    a lex:ReferenceEntry ;
    skos:prefLabel "legacy-properties" ;
    # source: http://example.org/ontology/tapi-streaming/stream-record/log-record/log-record-body/condition-detector/alarm-condition-detector-detail/legacy-properties
    skos:scopeNote "Alarm systems of the 20th century were based primarily on local lamps (initially filament bulbs) and bells.\nLamps can only be on or off, and bells sounding or not sounding, so alarms were Boolean in nature.\nWhere a detector was essentially multi-state it was converted into multiple Boolean statements.\nThe management of the equipments was essentially human only and local only (there were rarely remote systems).\nThe device with the problem was the only possible indicator of importance and it had only three distinct bulbs to illuminate (filament bulbs tend to fail requiring costly replacement).\nThe devices were relatively simple in function and analysis of the detectors was crude.\nThere was only the device to indicate severity\nThe device also could provide the best view as to whether a service was impacted, although clearly it had almost no knowledge.\nIn a modern solution with well-connected remote systems that increasingly analyze problems and where there is increasingly 'lights out' building operation, the device's guess at severity etc. is irrelevant.\nIn addition, with sophisticated resilience mechanisms, the device cannot make any relevant statement on whether the customer service has been impacted.\nLikewise, in a world where there were no remote systems and local management was the only practice, alarms had to be locally 'acknowledged'.\nWhere there are remote systems, per alarm acknowledge is burdensome.\nHowever, many solutions and operational practices continue to use the historic schemes.\nOn that basis, the schemes are supported but relegated to optional.\nCONDITION: Mandatory where legacy properties are to be conveyed." ;
    # source: http://example.org/ontology/grouping/tapi-streaming/legacy-properties
    skos:scopeNote "At this point in the evolution of control solutions LegacyProperties are probably mandatory, however, it is anticipated that as control solutions advance the LegacyProperties will become irrelevant." ;
    # source: http://example.org/ontology/grouping/tapi-streaming/legacy-properties
    skos:scopeNote "Grouping definition: legacy-properties" ;
    lex:canonicalExample "" ;
    lex:entityClass lex:GroupingKind ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/ontology/grouping/tapi-streaming/legacy-properties> ,
                        <http://example.org/ontology/tapi-streaming/stream-record/log-record/log-record-body/condition-detector/alarm-condition-detector-detail/legacy-properties> .

